Use PostgreSQL's built-in full-text search. At about 38,000 rows growing by a few hundred a month, a separate search service like Elasticsearch would add operational cost with no benefit.

**Schema:** add a generated, weighted `tsvector` column and a GIN index.

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search tsvector GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
) STORED;

CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY products_search_idx ON products USING gin (search);
```

The weights make a name match rank above a description match. The column stays in sync on its own, so no write path changes. On a table this size the rewrite is quick.

**Route:** add `GET /products/search?q=` in `routes/products.js`.

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const q = (req.query.q || "").trim();
  if (!q) return res.json([]);
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents
       FROM products, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1) query
      WHERE active AND search @@ query
      ORDER BY ts_rank(search, query) DESC, name
      LIMIT 50`,
    [q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

It must be registered before `/:id`. Otherwise Express treats `search` as an id.

`websearch_to_tsquery` accepts raw user input without throwing syntax errors, so there is no escaping to do. Paging can reuse the `OFFSET` approach from the listing route, which is fine at this scale.

**Decision to make first:** is this search-as-you-type, and does it need to tolerate typos? Full-text search matches whole words after stemming, so "scre" won't find "screwdriver" and "screwdrver" won't either. If the roadmap item means either of those, add a `pg_trgm` index on `name` and fall back to trigram similarity when the full-text query returns little. That is a separate piece of work, so tell me which one you mean before you plan it.
